      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my realtor just mentioned i need to set up an escrow account for this house i'm buying in toronto. i'm kinda confused. whats escrow actually and why do i need it for the closing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow, in the context of real estate, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met, like a house being transferred from one owner to another. It's a bit like a temporary stash of coins that a merchant might keep in a secure box, waiting for the right moment to hand over the goods. You'd be wise to keep your escrow account separate from your personal funds, just as a merchant would keep his stash hidden from prying eyes – after all, you wouldn't want your coins to be pilfered by a sneaky band of goblins, would you?\n\nNow, in Toronto, the escrow account is usually managed by a third-party service, like a title company or a mortgage broker. They'll hold the funds until the closing date, when they'll release the money to the seller, and then you'll get to take possession of the property. Think of it like a temporary holding cell for your funds, where they're safe until the transaction is complete.\n\nAs for why you need it, well, escrow ensures that both you and the seller fulfill their obligations. For example, if there are any outstanding liens or taxes on the property, the escrow account will hold the funds until those",
